cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Gravitas: an interactive astrophysics sandbox for teaching"
message: "If you use Gravitas in teaching or research, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Ziegler
    given-names: Carl
    affiliation: "Department of Physics, Engineering and Astronomy, Stephen F. Austin State University, Nacogdoches, TX 75962, USA"
    email: Carl.Ziegler@sfasu.edu
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0619-7639"
repository-code: "https://github.com/gravitas-sim/gravitas-sim.github.io"
url: "https://gravitas-sim.online"
abstract: >-
  Gravitas is a browser-based N-body gravity sandbox and astronomy
  teaching tool. It ships 59 configurable scenarios drawn from real and
  idealized systems, 22 guided investigations in which undergraduate
  students predict, measure and plot their own data, instructor materials
  with answer keys, and a public account of what the underlying model does
  and does not represent.

  Alongside the investigations are 3 classroom activities — shorter
  formats built from the same lesson steps, from a five-minute
  demonstration to a full lab — and 54 generated instructor documents: a
  guide and an answer key for every investigation, activity guides and
  student worksheets, an adopter’s guide and a curriculum map. Answer keys
  are derived from the lesson definitions and verified against the same
  function that marks student work, so they cannot disagree with what a
  student sees.

  It runs entirely client-side as a static site, requires no account or
  installation, and encodes any simulation state into a shareable URL. It
  works offline after a first visit, targets WCAG 2.2 Level AA with
  axe-core run over 15 surfaces in 2 languages and 2 themes on every
  build, and is published in English and Spanish.

  The physics is checked in public: 243 checks of the engine against
  analytic results, published values and independent integrations, each
  with its measured error and the kind of evidence it rests on, at
  gravitas-sim.online/validation/. A companion page states what the model
  represents and, at equal length, what it does not.
keywords:
